Camille Auer wanted to correct her gender, diagnosed as mentally ill, and now she's making art on the subject - 'People should be entitled to their own body'
Dozens of patient narratives hang from the ceiling of the Academy of Fine Arts' Project room gallery.
Each of them says Camille Auer's name.
The papers tell a perennial tale of how outside people have written down their interpretations of Auer and his identity.
They've decided for him who he is.
"I wanted to share my personal care history in order to become Violence in the process," Auer says.
By the process he refers to the gender reassignment process.
It's often a years-long course of research including meetings with psychologists, psychiatrists, nurses and doctors.
The process is initiated with a referral for "gender-identity disorder" diagnosis studies carried out in Tampere and in Helsinki.
For Auer, the process kicked off four years ago.
Even prior to this, he had used estrogen he had commissioned via the internet and was very advanced in his own repair process anyway.
He felt he was a woman, but according to Finnish law, a person has to go through the repair process in order to correct gender even in a legal manner.
The Trans Act, i.e. the Act on Confirmation of the Sex of Transcenegal (563/2002), obliges transgender people for forced sterilisation in order to bring the right gender into, for example, population registry centers and passports.
At the moment, a psychiatric diagnosment and proof of incapacitation are required of every gender.
A person has the right to decide their own bodies.
"This situation is intolerable and a violation of human rights," says Auer.
There has long been talk of reforming the Trans Act in Finland.
The United Nations Human Rights Council has issued eight receivables from the law and the European Court of Human Rights is a preceivable decision obliging the Parties to the European Convention of Human Rights to respect the right of self-determination.
Finland's previous government was preparing a legislative change under the leadership of Minister of Social Affairs Susanne Huovinen of Social Affairs and Social Affairs.
There was an election and the True Finns came to the government.
Now the change in the law is stalled again.
It's a matter of domestic political disputes that aren't even related to that law.
"The government doesn't want to put it on the table because there are disagreements within the government about changing the law," says Frank Johansson, Executive Director of Amnesty Finland.
Exterior of the exhibition space is dominated by two major installations.
One of them consists of the packaging and instructions for used medicines used by Auer.
In the Patient Report installation, any visitor can read, for example, the interpretation of a psychologist at Tampere University Hospital’s Transpolis from Auer.
The psychologist met Camille Auer twice and, based on these, wrote an account that would define Auer's right to care as well as assistance.
A sense of reality is prone to stabilisation.
In a boundary-level manner, the verguasing perception of oneself is fragmented and the unstable transcriptive transcriptions of personality are emphasized.
Auer didn't recognize himself from these descriptions.
"It was a shock to hear I was being diagnosed as mentally ill," Auer says.
There was an even greater shock when Auer wasn't granted gender reassignment.
Nursing staff said his resources weren't enough to start the process.
He was outlawed to have his own identity.
Then, on February 11, 2015, Auer walked out of the meeting and kicked a transpol waiting lounge for television.
Subsequently, a work of art called Monument for the Excluded was born, in which Auer refledds his experience of power and its use.
"It's crazy that any person defined as a woman at birth can magnifying their breasts, but you need a diagnosis to remove your breasts," Auer says.
Your body won't be yours if you're experiencing anything other than the amount of your gender determined at birth.
Auer’s experience isn't the only one of its kind.
The gender reassignment process is tougher for a number of mental reasons rather than for purely medical operations.
Those going through it will meet dozens of people who need to justify their own existence and their right to their own sexual experience.
"It's crazy that people who are in need of support and help are denied treatment, citing that their resources aren't enough," Auer says.
A number of people who have applied to the process have underlying mental symptoms: depressiveness, anxiety, possible bullying experiences.
Reliably, medical staff may provide that treatment cannot be started.
"Precitation of treatment and help can plunge a person into an even-worsening situation than it used to start the process," Auer says.
Auer's personal exhibition also deals with anxiety and thoughts of suicide.
Auer writes: "I've had suicidal thoughts throughout my adult life and it's usually hard for people to digest".
Determination has been treated with drugs and when Auer started to use hormones I felt relieved.
He'd finally been able to chant the sensations that had already existed in his childhood and teenage years.
Why a wide chin didn't seem to fit into your own body and why the pectoral muscles had to be round instead of a steady chest.
The most important thing for Auer has been that there's people close to him who can see him.
Being seen is a basic need.
"You'll have to just justify so much by talking, dressing, using the making of the sound and movements," he tells me.
By these days, Auer already finds himself to be seen, he exists as a woman.
In the early stages of the process, random comments from loved ones could hurt a lot.
It's most hurt when loved ones are wrong-gender.
"They should know and know who I am, but they won't confiscate or be able to internalize it," he continues.
But not all trans people need physical changes.
They don't suffer from body dysphoria, or confluential, where the body or its parts can cause anxiety and even physical pain.
However, without the use of hormones and a lengthy research process, you can't get official confirmation of your own gender.
"There's no medical basis for these studies," Auer says.
And it's true.
In June 2014, for example, Parliament adopted a new procedure for the recognition of legal sex.
The new legislation is based on self-deprecation in terms of legal gender.
A full-year-old applicant submits a written application for a new personal identification number, stating that he or she feels of another gender.
After a six-year cooling-of-threae period, the Applicancy confirms his/her application in writing and the ministry will provide a new personal identification number to the applicant.
This self-determination right is also demanded in Finland, and Auer’s exhibition is also encapsulated in this demand.
It hasn't been a therapy experience for the artist, it's a political and artistic collection of works.
I would like to believe that the art still carries the weight and will bring about political changes at a time when politicians have forgotten about human rights.
Camille Auer's exhibition Antiphallic Dick 4.-28.7.2017 at the Academy of Fine Arts Project Room.
A civil rights organization issued a travel warning to Missouri
The National NAACP, which advocates civil rights for Afro-American civil rights, has issued a travel warning on the Missouri, shaping state discriminatory practices and racist attacks there.
"NAACP's travel announcement for Missouri will enter into force on 28 August 2017, calling on African-Ufrica-American tourists, visitors and locals to pay particular attention and to exercise extreme caution when travelling in a state where there have recently been a number of questionable race-based incidents," a statement from the organization said in a statement.
The law, which recently came into force in Missouri complicates the export of anti-discrimination cases to the right. In addition tranche, the enforcement of state laws trajectories is irrigated against minorities. According to the NAACP, these factors led to the issue of a travel warning.
"Human civil rights will be violated.
They will be halted based on the color of their skin and assaulted or killed," Rod Chapel, president of the NAACP Missouri, told Kansas City Star.
"We haven't heard of as many incidents in as short times before."
The Missouri travel bulletin is the organization's first U.S. state warning.
The organization cited racist comments against African-American students at the University of Missouri, as well as the 28-year-old Black Tory Sanders from Tennessee.
Sanders died under dubious circumstances earlier this year. He was driving through the state when his car ran out of fuel. Missouri police detained him, although he wasn't prosecuted.
The travel bulletin also citing a Missouri Attorney's Inquest of a Missouri Attorney Clarifying officer 75 percent more likely to stop a black driver than white.
"The purpose of the tusslative bulletin is to dictate people to be wary of their families, friends and co-workers about what could happen in Missouri," Chapel says.
"Threateners have to prepare, for example, by contributing money to pay guarantees or telling relatives that they intend to travel through the state."
According to the F.B., 100 Hate Crimes were recorded in Missouri in 2015, i.e. the sixteenth highest number of U.S. states in the United States.
The travel warning is also a response to a new Missouri law that makes it difficult to sue companies for discrimination in housing and employment relationships.
ACLU, an organization that defends civil rights guaranteed by the Constitution recently issued travel warnings to Texas and Arizona after accepting immigration laws obliging the police to deregulate people for immigration violations. According to the organization, this will increase race-based profiling.
Travel warnings are usually issued by the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and apply to other states, but have recently been published by non-governmental organisations in response to certain laws and developments in the United States.
