Poverty and Mortgage in Indonesia's Golden Country
Bardina Degei doesn't use a stove for cooking.
He rarely wears a boiler.
Degei lives in a wooden building in Enarotal, the capital of the Pania region, located in Indonesia's unsettling province of Papua. When he prepares dinner, he tends to put a sweet potato – locally "nor" directly to a fireplace.
Within half an hour, a sweet potato that has been burnt to crouches is eaten voraciously with bare, unwashed hands.
There are no furnishings in Degei's home. He sits with a muddy-matter, where he usually handles his daily chores, such as a murky turbulent-in-cheat water of laundry.
A toilet post is provided by a bucket in a cathooting room.
Degei's the youngest of her husband's four wives, and they haven't been assigned fields to treat him.
Polygamy is common here.
Late, working can be dangerous: the vast majority of men in the village are out of the job, and a lot of people drink a lot.
"No one dare to walk in the village after 5 p.m. tonight," Degei says.
This is a rare glimpse into everyday life in Papua highlands, a former Dutch colony that was incorporated into Indonesia in 1969, preceded by a controversial referenrade referendum of 1,026 village invancence to vote in front of occupied forces.
A local independence movement opposing Dutch power quickly turned its anger into Jakarta's government, keeping the region under its strict control and preventing foreign journalists and human rights observers from entering it.
In 2003, the province was officially allocated to Papua and Western Papua. The eastern part of the island belongs to Papua New Guinea.
Enarotali is secluded and bleak. The journey there starts on an hour and a half flight from the provincial capital Jayapura to Nabire and continues with a belly-twishing five-throttle shaving on a rented car.
We don't have public transport here.
A town of about 19,000 people have wooden houses surrounded by bam-amboo fences whose corrugated tin roofs have stained to yellow brown.
Few Indonesians have been here, not to mention reporters and foreigners.
Prior to the arrival of Christian missionaries, Papuan Mee Pago tribalists adored Uga Tamee.
There have also been other changes.
"We didn't have clothes like this," Degei tells me to his colourful, hand woven threaded headgear, its dark fringes and a bright-coloured blouse.
"Before we only had magazines to cover our bodies."
Papua ranks Indonesia's poorest province. Twenty-eight per cent of its inhabitants live below the poverty line, and child mortality and illiteracy are among the highest in Asia.
But it's also an Indonesian gold country. - Yeah.
Paniai is an uptick in the size of New Jersey with a population of 15.3 000. Only about 100 kilometres from Pania is Grasberg, the world's largest and most lucrative gold mine. It's owned by Freeport McMoran, based in the Phoenix United States.
In 2015, the mine produced quid to Freeport worth tune of tune of tune of n.3.1 billion in gold.
Papua also has an estimated tune of wollen bucks of wollen.
For Papuans, however, these misfortunes bring turmoil, as they mean the stifling presence of Indonesia's strong army.
The New York Times revealed in 2005 that Freeport paid nearly bucks 20 million to local military personnel and military local units between 1998 and 2004. Individual officers were paid up to n150,000.
Papua demands for greater self-determination threaten this source of income and are merciless.
According to human rights activists, more than 5,500,000 Papuans have been killed by the Indonesian army since 1969 and raped, tormented and jailed thousands.
The Asian Human Rights Commission said the mass killings in Papua tribal highlands in the 1970s gained proportions of genocide.
Last year, more than 3,900 people were detained by Indonesian police in the region during peaceful protests.
Brisbane Archdiocese Report We Will Lose Everything of 2016 contains accounts of previous year's atrocities such as extrajurisdiction, torture – rape and electrocutions are particularly popular means – and reluctant attacks towards peaceful demonstrations.
"It's difficult to calculate the number of victims because there are incidents on a weekly basis," says Andreas Harsono, an Indonesian researcher at Human Rights Watch.
Screes have been tightened as Papua's natural resources attract migrants from other parts of Indonesia.
Eighty-three per cent of the province's 3.5-million-dollar residents are Christian, but the demographics are shifting as Indonesia's populous Java, Borneo, Sumatran and Sulawesi islands move to the region in hopes of better livelihood.
Javan warung stalls sell fried chicken and gado gado vegetable mix in peanut sauce.
It's hard for the locals to compete.
