New results of Swedish asylum-seeker age tests announced
The latest results of medical age tests for Swedish asylum seekers have been made public. The results of tests carried out using the new evaluation method reveal that more than 80 per cent of those tested were determined to be over the age of 18. However, not everyone considers the tests to be trustworthy.
Swedish National Forensic Forensics Agency began testing earlier this year.
A new test was put in place after the Swedish Immigration Office was criticised for failing to determine the true age of applicants who claimed to be minors.
The Finnish Immigration Service has so far commissioned 4,880 tests, and the Forensic Science Agency has now published results of 2,481 tests. The tests were carried out between mid-March and the end of July in 2017.
80 per cent of those tested, i.e. in 2002, an asylum seeker was determined to be over 18 years of age. In addition, the Forensic Medicine Agency determined in 25 cases that the person being tested was "possibly an 18-year-old or a parent".
In 432 cases it came to the conclusion that an asylum seeker is "possibly under 18 years of age."
Examinations were carried out only in cases of reason to suspect the age indicated by the applicant.
Johan Göransson of Forensic Medicine says the results are in line with results from previous months.
"Changes are fairly consistent. There are no major differences between the statistics of previous months," he told The Local newspaper.
Although a large number of applicants were determined to be more than 18-year-to-year-old, it doesn't necessarily implied that applicants have lioved their age.
In May, the agency unleased 5,181 test results, stating that 4,42, there were "indications to be examined to be 18-year-old or older."
When Svenska Dagbladet examined the same results, it became apparent that in 243 cases the applicant had openly stated that it was turning 18 this year.
The medical age assessment method used in tests takes X-ray images of wisdom tooth and an MRI images of knee joints. Then dentists and radiologists analyze the images. The method has also been criticized.
One sceptics is Claude Marcus, an endocrinologist at the Karolinitis Institute, whose work involves assessing people's biological ripeness. An assessment can determine whether a person has a puberty earlier than usual or later.
"I've had a reason to think about it, and I don't think it's the right method.
Determining biological age in this way is very precarious.
“Unless we can’t determine age, we shouldn't pretend to be able to do it,” he told the Metro newspaper.
Göransson of the Forensic Medical Agency, however, defended the accuracy of tests: "Unfortunately you can say the risk of determining a child to an adult is low.
There is the greatest risk in a situation where the person to be tested will soon be 18 years of age. The risk is about 10 per cent."
