PSG will have no trouble repaying Neymar's transfer amount and attaching him to his star arc, as the club will be funded by a dynasty dynasty who have been enriched with Qatar's oil.
Clubs are allowed to spend large sums on player acquisition but need to be rebalancing with real sources of revenue to avoid a loss-raising business associated with football.
Fair-game financial rules were drawn up at the initiative of Michel Platini, a Frenchman elected president of UEFA in 2007.
As a former soccer player, Platini took the view that the quest for success by indebtedness was cheating and led to serious problems if banks or owners took their support.
In 2011, the focus of fair-to-play financial rules shifted from the liabilities of clubs to monitoring their losses.
The change meant realization of Roman Abramovich's business model at Chelsea for the first time in 2003 was significantly hampered. Abramovich bought a promising squad, spent huge sums on player acquisitions and created a global brand.
The current three-year assessment period of fair-game financial rules lasts until 2018. During which clubs are allowed to make a loss of no more than €30 million.
Although Platini was forced to step down as UEFA president in a scandalous in 2015, his successor Aleksander Cefer also wants to curb retrieving clubs' reckless spending.
"Fair game rules will continue to be in place and will continue to apply them in the future," Andrea Traverso, in charge of fair-game financial rules at UEFA, told the Associated Press news agency last week.
Although PSG paid Neymar out of its contract with a lump sum, it can amortize the amount in accounting for the entire duration of his new contract.
Neymar has entered into a five-year contract, so the transfer amount can be recorded at an annual cost of €444.4 million for the next five years.
According to fair-game financial rules, first sanctions were handed down in 2014. The biggest sanctions were imposed on PSG and Manchester City funded from Abu Dhabi.
PSPG received a €60m fine later reduced to €20m. The club was also ordered to limit its Champions League group to 21 players for the 2014-2015 season instead of the usual 25 players.
UEFA is watching clubs who may be trying to trudge its financial regulations by increasing their income through companies associated with club owners.
For example, if a Qatarian company subsidise PSG but the value of the aid is considered to be higher than the normal level of the market, UEFA will not calculate the aid as actual results.
This was the case when UEFA decided in 2014 that the Qatari Tourism Authority's support for PSG was excessive.
It has also come to light that some of Neymar's merit at PSG could be funded by payments available from Qatar's 20-22 Soccer World Cup organizing committee.
PSG and the Arrangement Committee, however, have denied that Neymar is about to become an envoy for the World Championships; it would be very easy for UEFA to reveal such an arrangement.
Because of PSG's previous sanctions, UEFA is even more closely monitoring of attempts to traverse the rules than usual.
Sources familiar with the situation said on Thursday that UEFA has not been renegotiating with PSG of Neymar's relocation.
Sources gave an interview to the Associated Press news agency on the condition that their identities will not be disclosed because they do not have the authority to discuss PSG's situation.
According to the latest published financial statements, PSG’s net sales for the period 2015-2016 amounted to EUR 542.44 million and an operating profit of more than EUR 10 million.
The financial statements for last period have not yet been published.
PSPG expect Neymar's Arriving to gravitably to attract new sponsors to boost sales of club products and staging jerks.
The club estimates that its turnover will grow by at least 30 per cent under Neymar's agreement.
The Spanish league, clearly emblazed from the departure of the world's best player, tried to complicate Neymar's move. It was alleged to break fair-game financial rules.
"It's a definite economic do-publishing case for both the club and the state," Spanish league president Javier Tebas told the Spanish newspaper AS. By state he referred to PSG's qatarian ownership.
Arsenal manager Arsene Wenger is also wary of the state funding the activities of a football club.
"The case is the result of ownership that has completely altered football's operating environment over the last 15 years," Wenger said on Thursday.
"Anything is possible after the state owns a soccer team.
Precizing fair-to-game financial rules will be very difficult. There are so many interests associated with a famous player representing the state."
