A majority of Syria's refugee youth don't think they're going to return to their homeland on a permanent basis unless the war ends and ISIS leaves the country
A Voice for Young Syrian Refugees surveyed the opinions and attitudes of young Syrian refugees residing in Jordan and Lebanon.
Syrian refugees basking bread in a refugee camp in Jordan.
A majority of Syrian youth don't think they're going to re-emergence permanently.
According to them, the end of the war in the Syrian war and the departure of I ISIS are the most important things that should happen to be able to return to their country of origin.
You don't see the Trump administration bring a change to the Syrian conflict.
Burson-Marsteller, a communications office chain, surveyed the opinions and attitudes of young Syrian refugees in an international study.
Burson-Marsteller has spent nine years studying the opinions and attitudes of Arab adolescence.
This year, the A Voice for Young Syrian Refugees study examined the opinions and attitudes of young Syrian refugees residing in Jordan and Lebanon.
Between February-March 2017, a total of 400 Syrian youth aged between 18 and 24 responded to the survey, of whom half are women and half are men.
Half of the interviews were conducted in Jordan and half in Lebanon.
According to the study, more than half of young Syrian refugees don't think they'll return to their homeland permanently in the future.
Forty-two per cent of respondents can think they will return to their home country and four per cent couldn't say.
According to the study, young people's decision to return to their home is most affected by the ongoing war in Syria, as well as the presence of ISIS in the country.
Nearly half (47%) see the end of the war for permanent repatriation as the most impressive factor, while a quarter think the most important thing would be ISIS leaving the country.
Only less than one-tenth highlight the improvement of the economic situation or Bashar al-Assad's departure from Syria.
Milital action alone won't solve conflict
Syrian youths were asked which factor is most important to end the conflict.
The vast majority think the end of the war is more important than the abdication of President Bashar al-Assad.
More than a quarter see al-Assad's presidency as an obstacle to a peace deal.
Nearly half feel there is a need for both political and military solutions to put an end to the ongoing Syrian conflict.
To resolve the conflict through mere military action, you don't believe more than a good quarter.
A majority of Syrian refugee leaders see ISIS power weakened over the past year, while one-fifth feel its power has been strengthened.
Respondents say the most important aspect of the fight against ISIS, and in general, is that the media convey to people information that ISIS has nothing to do with Islam.
Nearly just as many people made military measures and education reform a priority.
The most important thing would be to create better-paid jobs for young people.
Asked about the Trump regime's impact on the Syrian conflict, 66 per cent of respondents believe that the conflict will not change under the Trump administration.
Nearly one in four believe the Syrian conflict will worsen during Trump's term.
