A day earlier, he had arrived in New York from his home in St. Louis and was sitting on the bed of his airbnb apartment scrolling Facebook on his phone.
That's when he spotted a message from his high school friend:
"Police just killed an 18-year-old youth, and his body is still in the street."
Confusmably, he noted that no one else had posted the same information on his feed. That's why he shut down Facebook and opened Twitter.
There were numerous retwesome re-tweets on his timeline from TheePharoah.
The username belongs to a rapper based in St. Louis who tweeted in real time about Michael Brown's death. A white police officer had fired an unarmed 18-year-old black Brown in Ferguson, Missouri.
The user in question had also posted a picture of Brown's inanivaged corpses on the street, where it was dumped for four hours.
"It's unbelievable," Ferrell thought.
“Death of death was nothing new to him and his community.
When he went back to Twitter that night, there were reports of people flocking to Canfield Drive.
Users posted images of police cordoning tapes and screaming people, as well as a man who claimed to be Brown's father who claimed to be a sign reading: "Ferguson police just killed my unarmed son!"
Ferrell followed a real-fashioned video footage of police standing in front of demonstrators with a barking dog.
He says he has also seen a video of another officer tweaking his rifle and shouted, "Leave all of you... animals."
"This isn't normal," Ferrell remembers stating.
'It wasn't the kind of murder we've already got used to and could be described as typical.
That's when I felt some cold colors.
I was angry and I was crouching.”
He drafted a return flight at home for the next day.
Ferrell went to get his six-year-old daughter from his father's house and decided to go to Ferguson the next morning.
What happened to Ferrell and Ferguson over the next nearly three-year period has been the subject of a documentary "Whose Streets?" which premiered in cinemas on August 11, 2017.
The film is the story of survival and protesting, love and loss of strength and resilientness from the point of view of local people who took to the streets to demand answers.
When Ferrell pushed towards Ferguson with his daughter, dressed in a floral dress and a crown compatible with it, they had a re-conflict discussion about "what it's like to live in blackness and in whatever circumstances." They had spoken countless times about it before that morning.
"Will you remember when I told you how the Black people have had to fight for the things they believe in?" Ferrell remembers saying to his daughter.
"We're going to Ferguson because a cop killed an 18-year-old boy, and it's not right."
"I couldn't ignore my daughter," Ferrell later told me.
"The events of today are the culmination of past events.
My daughter has the right to know and grow into it.
He needs to be equipped for activism and organisation, but he must also know the different layers and circumstances of the experience of living in black. And he needs to know what we need to do to free ourselves.
Fortunately, even though I've been able to protect him from blatant racism doesn't mean he's just like other black girls.
I'll feel like I'd hurt him if I protected him from reality.'
Their first staging point in Ferguson in Ferguson was the South Florissant Road, located across from a police station opposite a police station. It became a campplace of activists when they were awaiting information about the handling of the case and eventually found out there would be no prosecution against the police.
From there, Ferrell and his daughter went out to help a local entrepreneur who made lunch packages.
They froze lunches in bags and distributed them to demonstrators.
Ferrell gradually began to view activism in a new way.
"My activism changed completely since August 9," he tells me. While studying at the University of Missouri in St. Louis, Ferrell was president of an organization of nursing students belonging to minorities. At the time, he focused on food rights and health inequalities in black communities.
“I didn’t have the experience of organizing.
“It just happened.”
The weeks later Ferrell met Sabaah Folayan at an evening rally.
Folayan said he and cinematographer Lucas Alvarado-Fararrar "movably wanted to tell me what was going to happen.
Ferrell's miscalculation asked, "Do you really want to tell what's going to happen, or do you want a story you can take advantage of?"
Suspicion was prompted by those countless times when people showed up in a local community, usually white people who just wanted to benefit from its difficulties and perseverance.
"We'd heard those born at Ferguson didn't have a common voice," Folayan said in a recent interview. "We wanted to do things differently."
Folayan had already contacted deputy instructor Damon Davis, known for activism among death-minded incarnated incarnations.
Davis' involvement prompted Ferrell to join the project along with six other locals. The film crew followed the events in the years following the Brown shooting.
"This isn't a document where someone speaks to us or speaks for us, we're going to speak," Davis says.
"The most important thing for me was how local people are portrayed in a movie because I'm portrayed in the same way."
According to Folayan, it wasn't to focus on Ferrell's experiences because there was a desire to show that the popular movement "is not about a charismatic leader but a community." Ferrell nonetheless turned out to be the most open to describe.
That's why the documentary features surprisingly private aspects of Ferrell's life, such as falling in love and getting married.
"I felt pretty naked," Ferrell culminates as she told her what it felt like to see a movie for the first time.
"But I understood that this work and the human depiction of blacks require a certain vulnerability."
Additionally, Ferrell's story recounts and refocuses the role of black queer women in the broader Black Lives Matter movement.
The movement was born on social media (BlackLivesMatter) after a police officer shot a black teenage boy, Trayvon Martin, in 2012. Of the movement's three founders, identified with queer identity.
"Black queer women's leadership has kept the whole movement going," Ferrell says. "It's because we're going to have to fight constantly on many fronts.
"Black queer women have had to endure everything."
Folayan agrees: "Moscoat really is maintained by black women, among whom there are many queer-women among them."
However, he underscored Ferrell was uneltected in the documentary "intentally" because of his queer identity.
"He got the energy mobilized," says Folayan.
"But it's no coincidental that she was on the front line. Black queer women live at the cutting point of many things, and they've got a lot at stake.
His energy is shuffled from the big screen.”
Ferguson has been dumped by news filming crews a long time ago, but relations between the community and the police remain tense.
Ferrell has two and a half years of probation left. He was sentenced for blocking a freeway at a demonstration.
His description of the social political climate in his neighborhood is brief and succincible: "Mixed."
Yet he still relied on the slogan he memorized in the streets of Ferguson three years ago:
“It’s our responsibility to fight for our freedom.
It's our obligation to win.
We have to gravitate and support each other.
We can only lose our shudder.'
