Similar to Glint, Convercent allows companies to conduct customized queries, collect answers confidentially in real-time, and identify problem areas.
In addition, employees can report inaccurate behaviour anonymously via text message.
And if there's any reason to suspect a manager of inunpriviation, the complaints go directly to the company's board.
"A general opinion has supplanned authorities," said Patrick Quinlan, Convercent's founder and managing director.
He reminds us that if a company mistreats its employees or acts unethically, the consequences could be costly even if the authorities do not intervene.
The Ruby Tuesday restaurant chain has more than 25,000 employees at 500 locations. The company has used Convercent for more than a year to ensure that employees are aware of operating principles and procedures and are able to easily connect to headquarters.
Earlier, it was the practice that if an employee wanted to report a problem, they first had to find out a phone number or an e-mail address to make a notification to the head office.
"It was often a daunting and uninteresting experience," says James Vitrano, Ruby Tuesday's General Counsel. He said it was difficult to monitor complaints made by employees.
Earlier problems remained hidden from leaders working in another city or state. Now problems such as discrimination, harassment and unjust wages can be detected and handled more quickly.
"I'll get a more holistic and comprehensive understanding of the employee's reality," says Vitrano, which leads Ruby Tuesday's risk management team.
"At the same time, we will protect our shareholders from employment-related litigations."
Quinnlan said companies began to address ethics, values and employee engagement even more seriously after auditing firm Arthur Andersen plummeted as a result of unethical operations in connection with the Enron scandal.
It wasn't it with social media that companies realized they couldn't stop public sorting of their dirty laundry anymore.
"Holiuld was used as a strategy before the technology came to controlling ethics," says Quinlan.
Glint and Convercent provide their software as a service that is regularly deployed.
It's a business model and an opportunity approved by venture capitalists. Both Glint and Convercent are backed by venture capitalists.
