Experts in workplace culture say information about employee engagement is important.
But it is useless if no action is taken.
If a company requests feedback from its employees, it can be seen as a sign of change.
And if the change doesn't come quickly enough or there's nothing to change, disappointment can reduce employee engagement.
"If you're requesting 4,000 submissions, you'll have to be prepared to have four thousand conversations," Russell Raath, president of Kotter International, a management consultancy firm who has seen companies relying too much to collect information alone.
"If a company doesn't take any action, employees will begin to wonder if they're heard
Do their opinions really matter.
“Wheasing the company doesn’t they care?”
Errett says Madison Reed currently conducts staff surveys on a monthly basis and is able to tackle the problem on the same day it's reported at Glint.
When Errett had garnered feedback from fruscitated employees who were frustrated with decision-making, Errett was able to make changes to teams and provide communication training to solve problems.
Ruby Tuesday understands its employees even better and hopes a deeper understanding will help it to improve employee retention in a long-term industry known for high employee turnover.
"If a company doesn't commit to creating a culture of transparency, it's going to lose workers," says Vitrano.
"If it loses employees, it'll also lose customers.
“There are no companies without customers.”
