"I feel a personal connection when I hold a letter in my hand."
Rhodes' book, A Castle in England, tells the stories of families who lived in Scotney from the Middle Ages to an Edwardian era. Each story is illustrated by a variety of artists, promising British cartoonist: Isaac Lenkiewicz, Briony May Smith, William Exley, Becky Palmer or Isabel Greenberg.
This isn't Rhodes's first investigative book. In 2014, Dead Men's Teeth and Other Stories, whose sources of inspiration were documents of the British National Library. The project was stimulant.
"I moved to tears when I noticed a thumbprint in a letter. The letter had been written by a double agent who worked for the Jacobites in the 18th century," Rhodes says.
"It was his fingerprint!
And the letters fragrant from smoke because at that time fire was used as illumination.
Three centuries later the fragrance makes the reader feel like he's on the spot.
“I love the archives.”
A writer attracted to old documents was able to live at the castle for almost four months with the support of the National Trust Foundation and the English National Art Council, which seemed too good to be true. He admits he couldn't stay in the castle for a little longer than would have been hoped.
