Scotney's closer history is quite unfamilient, as the castle went entirely to the National Confessor only after Elizabeth Hussey died in 2006.
When Rhodes arrived about ten years later, the foundation's staff were just starting to go through the archive. Rhodes describes that the archives had been kept for hundreds of years using the same method: by taking everything to an attic.
There were diaries in the archives, letters from English empire days and maps of who owned what. "You could imagine that land disputes are boring," Rhodes says.
It occurred to me that I think it should be.”
Some of the stories in the book are proceeding from the castle to the history of England, such as The Labourer. It's about a man associated shortly after graduation of the castle into a peasant rebellion in 1381.
"Knightly Families of Kent and Sussex cites a rioting at the castle in 1380, Rhodes says.
"England's peasant revolt began in the same area, and it occurred to me that revolt can begin with a riot.
"Perhaps these events are linked to each other."
The book's other stories are closely related to the history of the castle, such as The Priest, the story of a priest hike by Darrell's family for seven years during the English Reformation.
The Smuggler, on the other hand, recounts the taunt of smuggler Arthur Darrell of the 18th century. He is said to have staged his own funeral with a creaking of a creasks with a creasks (this was discovered years later when his tomb was unlocked).
Darrels are Rhodes' favorite family in Scotney. "The men in the family seem to have been quite stalkers. They kept getting into debt and buying things they couldn't afford.
Darrell's family men solved their debt problems by marrying wealthy old-wielding women. I see them as handsome buddies who knew how to court heirs."
Rhodes is looking for new writing experiences.
He celebrated two weeks in a comic book written in Finland for a co-mortgage of a sanatorium operating at the beginning of the 20th-threaten sanatorium.
Scotney Castle has a special place in his heart.
"It was sad to leave there," he tells me.
