GMB is concerned about the continued operation of McVitie's McVitie's Scottish plant
The federation is picking up reinforcements from McVitie's Inventive Inventor Manufacturers for long-term commitment to production at the Glasgow plant.
According to the UK General Trade Union (GMB), the company has not disclosed its plans for the future of the Tollcross plant.
There are more than 500 employees at the Hobnobs and Rich Tea biscuits and other products.
Pladis, which owns the McVitie's brand, said it had invested more than quip quip  pound in the factory in recent years.
The company added that employees and trade union representatives will certainly be "predicted first" if there are changes to operations.
In a letter to the factory manager, the GMB urges the company to "equivalent to worry that no funding has been allocated to modernization of production lines."
Drew Duffy of GMB trade union attaches great concern to the lack of clear information about Tollcross's business needs and future investment plans.
"Family have worked from one generation to the next here, and the workforce is heavily concentrated in the local community and the Glasgow metropolitan area. It is a major source of employment in the area of high levels of unemployment and low livelihood.
From the point of view of jobs, salaries and subsistence of Brexit, rising cost of living and continuous resemdication of production, eastern Glasgow urgently needs successful McVitie's production."
Pladis spokeswoman said the factory manager had only received a trade union's letter the day before and the company responds directly to the letter because it appreciates constant interactions with employees and trade union representatives.
"We had a regular meeting with employees and GMB representatives at Tollcross two months ago, when we talked openly about a number of issues.
We discussed, among other things, with other highly competitive and pregnased market-based companies to continuously assess all of our businesses and offices in order to maintain our competitiveness."
A spokeswoman says the company has invested more than GBP 5 million in the Tollcross factory in recent years, particularly in its production lines and improving operations.
"We are aware of Tollcross's long history as a manufacturing production facility and we'll be the first to tell employees and trade union representatives if we're going to make any changes to factory operations."
